Troubleshooting
Issue: Nginx fails to start

Solution: Check configuration with sudo nginx -t
Verify port 80 is not in use: sudo netstat -tlnp | grep :80
Issue: Permission denied errors

Solution: Ensure scripts have execute permissions: chmod +x script.sh
Check sudo access for deployment operations
Issue: Backup directory not found

Solution: Verify path in script matches actual directory
Create manually: mkdir -p ~/deployment-lab/backups
Issue: Git checkout fails

Solution: Ensure you're in the correct directory
Check available tags: git tag -l
Conclusion
You have successfully implemented a rollback procedure system that includes:

Version-controlled application deployments using Git tags
Automated backup creation before deployments
Rollback capability to restore previous working versions
Verification procedures to confirm deployment status
Audit logging for tracking rollback events
These skills are critical for production environments where deployment failures must be quickly remediated. Rollback procedures minimize downtime and provide a safety net for deployment operations. In real-world scenarios, you would extend this with additional features like database rollbacks, configuration management, and automated health checks.

Key Takeaways:

Always create backups before deployments
Use version control tags for release management
Implement verification steps to detect failures quickly
Maintain audit logs for compliance and troubleshooting
Test rollback procedures regularly in non-production 
